<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acrocorinth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Acrocorinth is the steep rock, about 575 metres high, that rises above ancient Corinth in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. It served as the city's acropolis, with a temple of Aphrodite on its summit, and as one of the strongest fortresses in Greece, controlling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a>. Held in turn by Macedonians, the Achaean League, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, Venetians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, it preserves walls from many periods.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acrocorinth.jpg" alt="Acrocorinth" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: elveoflight · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akrokorinth_nordmauern.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 243 BCE Aratus captured <b>Acrocorinth</b> from its Macedonian garrison in a night assault, and Corinth, Megara, Troezen and Epidaurus joined.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Leo Sgouros held <b>Acrocorinth</b> until his death in 1208, Corinth fell in 1210 and Argos and Nauplia in 1212, while Venice kept the ports of Modon and Coron in the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Corinth grew in the early Iron Age on the Isthmus linking the Peloponnese to central Greece, below the fortress hill of <b>Acrocorinth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a></li><li><blockquote>He tried to block Boniface of Montferrat at Thermopylae in 1204 but retreated to the near-impregnable citadel of <b>Acrocorinth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-leo-sgouros">Lordship of Leo Sgouros</a></li></ul></section>
